    {
      "id": "public-arrival-template-landing",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Public Arrival Template",
      "root": true,
      "position": {
        "x": -200,
        "y": -100
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 240,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "public-arrival-template"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Public Arrival Template\n\nA public root is an orientation problem before it is a relationship problem.\n\nSemantic edges flow from the **bottom of a parent** to the **top of a child**. When a node exposes multiple downward relationships, those bottom handles are spread across the lower edge with distinct angles rather than collapsed onto the center."
        }
      }
    },
